tyler: heads up — hitting a weird auth + retry failure when calling the JS SDK from Next middleware (Vercel edge). Works fine in serverless API route but in middleware the token refresh trashes the request. stack: `TypeError: process is not defined` then `UnhandledPromiseRejection: Cannot set timer in Edge`

eva: ugh. can you paste the tiny repro? are you importing the normal SDK (cjs) or an edge-specific entrypoint? :thinking:

tyler: here's the minimal middleware:```js
import { createClient } from 'redwood-sdk'

export async function middleware(req) {
  const client = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_KEY })
  const r = await client.generate({ prompt: 'hi' })
  return new Response(r.text)
}
```
errors happen during module eval when createClient reads process.env and sets up retry timers. stack points to node-timers shim inside SDK.

ben: yeah that pattern hits edge constraints. middleware modules run in V8 isolates (no Node globals like process, setImmediate, sometimes timers behave differently). two quick fixes: 1) use the SDK's edge entrypoint ("redwood-sdk/edge") which avoids Node libs, or 2) lazy-init inside the handler and avoid reading process.env at import time. e.g.```js
export async function middleware(req) {
  const { createClient } = await import('redwood-sdk/edge')
  const client = createClient({ apiKey: req.headers.get('x-api-key') || '' })
  // retry/backoff is edge-aware here
}
```

ev a: also for TS people — watch types: importing `next/server` vs DOM Response can clash. add `/// <reference lib="dom" />` or install `@types/web` in the workspace so `Response`/`Request` types line up.

tyler: tried the `/edge` import but still got `ReferenceError: TextEncoder is not defined` (weird, Vercel usually polyfills). any other gotchas?

ben: TextEncoder comes from the global web-apis; on some Next versions you need to enable `experimental-edge` or use `globalThis.TextEncoder = require('util').TextEncoder` (not ideal). The edge entrypoint should avoid Node util usage — double-check you're pulling the right path and that bundler isn't resolving to the CJS file. ESM vs CJS interop can silently resolve wrong file. use `await import('redwood-sdk/edge')` to force ESM semantics.

tyler: ah right — bundler kept rewriting imports. switching to dynamic import fixed resolution. also moved token logic into middleware handler instead of module top-level. now the retry loop uses `AbortController` from global and behaves.

devex-bot: created issue 4321: "middleware + sdk: doc + example for edge entrypoint + TS types" <https://github.com/redwood-inference/sdk/issues/4321> :white_check_mark:

eva: i'll add a short sample under `examples/next-edge-auth` with TS typings and a comment about avoiding process.env at module load. should include both dynamic `await import()` and static `import('redwood-sdk/edge')` example.

tyler: thanks — that would save others. one more small note: if you rely on SDK auto-retries make sure you configure a short timeout or use a single-shot fallback in middleware (latency sensitive). our middleware has max 500ms budget so we switched to one try + fallback model. :thumbsup:

ben: good call. also logging tip: in edge use `console.info` but aggregate client-side traces to our tracing endpoint — middleware logs are short-lived in Vercel.

tyler: works now, i'll push the repro and link the issue. ping me if you want to review the sample.

eva: thanks! we should also add a short note in the SDK readme about "no top-level env reads" for edge.

tyler: agreed. closing here after PR.
